TY  - CHAP
T1  - Practical Natural Language Processing: The REL System as Prototype
AU  - Thompson, Frederick B.
AU  - Thompson, Bozena Henisz
A2  - Rubinoff, Morris
A2  - Yovits, Marshall C.
BT  - Advances in Computers
PB  - Elsevier
VL  - 13
SP  - 109
EP  - 168
PY  - 1975
DA  - 1975/01/01/
SN  - 0065-2458
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/S0065-2458(08)60657-9
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0065245808606579
AB  - Publisher Summary
A lot has been written and much work has been done on natural language as a means of communication with computers. Some of the problems involved in the development of systems with such a capability have been effectively solved; others, more difficult, have been more clearly delineated. It is now worthwhile to assess the current state of the art and future directions in this area of research. The interest here is to achieve an early operational system for natural language communication with computers. This chapter reflects this concern. The chapter constantly focuses on the question: What can be done now? The views here are based upon direct and successful experience with the problems discussed. The system for communicating with the computer in natural language discussed here, the REL (Rapidly Extensible Language) System, is in experimental operation and is nearing the transportable, prototype stage. This chapter calls on examples from this work to make the points clear and concrete. The question that is sought to be answered in this chapter is: How can a qualified professional effectively use the computer for relevant research with minimum constraints on the language of communication?
ER  - 

